<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lambityeco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lambityeco is an archaeological site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, near Tlacolula, occupied mainly between about 600 and 800 CE as Monte Albán declined. A Zapotec town that produced salt, it is known for its well-preserved elite residences, tombs and stucco friezes and masks, including large heads of the rain god Cocijo and portraits of a noble couple. It was abandoned around 800 CE, while nearby centres such as Mitla grew in importance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lambityeco.jpg" alt="Lambityeco" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ivanpares · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lambityeco.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
